<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cytokinin-O-glucosides biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2902" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Glucosylation of cytokinins is a well recognized modification that is thought to play an important role in hormonal homeostasis. Several types of cytokinins conjugation exist which render cytokinins inactive: &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-xylosylation, &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation, and &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation. Zeatin-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside occurs in all plants examined and is thought to be important in transport |CITS:[LETHAM94]|, storage |CITS:[LETHAM94]|, and protection against zeatin oxidases |CITS:[4214594], [ARMSTRONG94]|. These suggested role are supported by the findings that &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation is reversible (these conjugates can be converted back into active cytokinins by &amp;beta;-glucosidases |CITS: [8235622]|), and the &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosides are resistant to cleavage of the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-side chain by cytokinin oxidases |CITS: [12721786]|. To date a number of genes encoding UDP-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyltransferases have been characterized in several plants. The genetic manipulation leading to controlled expression of those enzymes should allow a more precise assessment of the function of &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylated cytokinins in regulating growth and development |CITS:[9874810]|.</Description>
