    <DataNode elementId="AT1G22400-MONOMER" textLabel="UDP-glycosyltransferase 85A1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SK82" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="615" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The UDP glycosyltransferase (UGT) encoded by the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis UGT85A1&lt;/i&gt; catalyzes the formation of &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosides. The activity of enzyme was tested on a variety of cytokinins. Two interesting characteristics emerged from that study: i) in contrast to cytokinin-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycosyltransferases from other species |CITS:[11331778],[12644686]|, recombinant |FRAME:TAX-3701 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;| UGT85A1 catalyzes the glucosylation of both &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;- and &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-zeatin |CITS:[15342621]|; and ii) the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation of dihydrozeatin abolished activity of UGT85A1, implying that glucosylation at one site of the cytokinin strongly influences secondary glucosylations |CITS:[15342621]|.</Comment>
